they prayed first but their intent to  protest was evident  it wasn't long before they marched and  denounced the imminent visit of the  prime minister narendra modi  led by some of kolkata's leading  activists and religious leaders  the general public poured onto the  streets in their thousands  winding their way through calcutta's  busy shopping district  the ruling party's chief minister mps  and civil society have not confirmed  whether they're meeting the prime  minister  as he comes to celebrate the 150th  anniversary of the deep sea port  they're all objecting to the citizenship  law which was passed in december  seen as unjust and divisive the  government  is completely rattled that you can see  because  there's no end there is no leadership no  central leadership  people are calling their own but in  every single one of them you see hindus  and muslims together waving the national  flag  demanding that we won't allow us to be  divided so it's a very  particular kind of movement as i said i  have not seen such a movement  since mahatma gandhi death  but it doesn't end there this park is  hosting one of the many peaceful but  noisy protests  that have been continuing through the  week and in different parts of calcutta  and as day turns to night the voices of  protesters continue to be heard loud and  strong  across the city this particular  gathering of several hundred has been  ongoing for four consecutive nights  and their numbers are growing they've  come from all sections of society  many of them women recovering from  surgery usma jameel a housewife  feels strongly about the government's  moves to alienate muslims and other  minorities  and asked her friends and neighbours to  join her in this local park  to demonstrate before modi arrives each  evening this week  it's grown and morphed into a major  focal point for anti-government  sentiment  i hope the government understands we  reject the citizenship laws  we'll never submit our paperwork and we  look forward to the supreme court  hearing  because every indian is focused on that  date and our hope  is that those laws will be thrown out  across india people have taken to the  streets the parks and universities  to voice their discontent with laws they  perceive as injust  and racist here in calcutta they're  being led on a chilly winter evening  by a first-time activist usma rallied to  the cause with no other aim but to face  for her  an unjust law that could determine her  very existence  and that of millions of others across  india  sahil rahman al-jazeera calcutta
 

